Calculate service from shared support and component dependence (@engineering-service-dependencies: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/service/Maintenance-intervals-access-and-functional-evidence.lingua#r_0GJDWMZFSFC6Y6DYH6G07BP5F1") { r_0064D5D7B1AF09AA70DFF8597F
Two installed units are not two independent service paths if both require the same support. Let S, A and B be indicators of functioning support and two units at a specified observation time. One unit of service requires S=1 and at least one of A,B=1. Its indicator is S[1−(1−A)(1−B)], while two units of service require SAB. Draw the functional dependency before calculating probabilities.

In a stipulated snapshot model, S, A and B are independent with probabilities rₛ, rₐ and rᵦ. Then one-unit service probability is rₛ[1−(1−rₐ)(1−rᵦ)]. For rₛ=9/10 and rₐ=rᵦ=4/5 this is 108/125 = 0.864. Treating the complete paths SA and SB as independent instead gives 1−(1−0.72)² = 0.9216. Their covariance is rₐrᵦrₛ(1−rₛ)=0.0576, exactly the overstatement. Derive this by computing E[(SA)(SB)]−E[SA]E[SB], using S²=S. Installing another unit does not remove the common support limit: one-unit service probability can never exceed rₛ.

Without independent units, condition on support: P(service)=P(S=1){P(A=1|S=1)+P(B=1|S=1)−P(A=B=1|S=1)}. Marginal component reliabilities do not determine the intersection. If A and B always share the same condition, both with marginal probability 0.8, adding B contributes no redundancy. If repair work competes for one crew, the component states can become dependent even when primitive failure events were independent.

Availability also depends on the required output. In the independent snapshot example, two-unit service probability is 0.9×0.8² = 0.576. A station that is “available” for half demand may fail a full-demand requirement. The dynamic study therefore retains capacity as well as a binary adequate-service indicator.

**Work:** identify possible common supports in a benign workshop or document service: electricity, shared input material, a configuration file, a trained operator, an access route or a calibration reference. Identify an observation that distinguishes a failed unit from a failed support. Propose an alternative whose independence claim can actually be checked. Include duplicated maintenance work and added interfaces in the comparison; the snapshot formula alone is not a design recommendation.

**Continue:** [Maintenance intervals, access and functional evidence](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/service/Maintenance-intervals-access-and-functional-evidence.lingua#r_0GJDWMZFSFC6Y6DYH6G07BP5F1).

